WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.650
- Hi, everyone, I'm Maurice Samuels,

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:09.550
the director of the Yale Program

00:00:09.550 --> 00:00:11.340
for the study of Anti-Semitism,

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:14.120
and along with my colleague, Carolyn Dean,

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.580
we wanna welcome you here today.

00:00:16.580 --> 00:00:19.820
It's an especially, excuse me, great honor

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.930
to introduce today's speaker, Philip Nord.

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:25.530
He's the Rosengarten Professor of Modern

00:00:25.530 --> 00:00:27.670
and Contemporary History at Princeton,

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:30.360
where he's taught since 1981.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:33.040
One of the leading historians of late 19th

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:36.560
and 20th century France, excuse me.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.600
He's devoted his career
to exploring the working

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.670
of ideologies like
anti-Semitism and republicanism,

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:46.770
and how they shape cultural forms.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.310
Excuse me.

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:49.457
His books include,

00:00:49.457 --> 00:00:54.117
"Paris Shopkeepers and the
Politics of Resentment," 1986,

00:00:54.117 --> 00:00:57.200
"The Republican Moment:
Struggles for Democracy

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.107
in 19th Century France," 1995,

00:01:00.107 --> 00:01:03.200
"Impressionists and
Politics: Art and Democracy

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.650
in the 19th Century,"
2000, "France's New Deal:

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:09.750
From the '30s to the Postwar Era," 2010,

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:12.980
and his new book, which
we'll be talking about today.

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:17.470
After the Deportation: Memory
Battles in Postwar France,"

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:20.550
which was just published by
Cambridge University Press

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:23.510
and is the subject of a
round table on H-Diplo

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:25.260
that Carolyn edited.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.630
And I'll just say that
this book is stunning,

00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:30.150
in both its breadth and its depth.

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:32.240
As I said in my review
for that round table,

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.850
it's gonna be the definitive
history of the way

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:37.843
that World War II was
remembered in France.

00:01:38.717 --> 00:01:41.620
Philip Nord is a Guggenheim
fellow, a former director

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:43.280
of the Shelby Cullom Davis Center

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.210
for Historical Studies at Princeton,

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:48.100
and the recipient of
many awards and honors.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:51.299
He also happens to be a fellow Chicagoan.

00:01:51.299 --> 00:01:53.270
And so now let me just say a word

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:55.800
about how things will work today.

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:58.020
First, Phil will present the book,

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.830
then Carolyn is gonna
say a few words about it,

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:03.260
then we'll open it up to your questions

00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:06.260
and we'll finish promptly at 6:00 p.m.

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:09.910
So please submit your questions
using the Q&amp;A function

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:11.460
at the bottom of your screen.

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:14.620
And I will ask them to Phil.

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:17.500
Please don't raise your
hands since we won't see you.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:20.520
You're welcome to start
submitting questions at any time,

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.240
including during the lecture itself.

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:26.793
And now I will hand it over
to professor Philip Nord.

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:28.690
- Thank you, Maury.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:33.649
So greetings to the
audience whom I can't see,

00:02:33.649 --> 00:02:38.649
but I'm hopeful, if not
confident that you're out there.

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:43.910
Let me begin by expressing
some words of thanks

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:47.090
to Maurice Samuels and Carolyn Dean

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:49.260
for organizing this event.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:53.600
And also a word also for Alice Kaplan,

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:56.390
who was supposed to
participate but has been ill.

00:02:56.390 --> 00:02:59.590
And so I'd like to wish her well.

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:03.270
So let me begin with a
few introductory remarks,

00:03:03.270 --> 00:03:06.140
before we turn to the discussion.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:10.670
About 160,000 French people
were deported from France

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:13.730
during World War II to Nazi camps

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.580
in Central and Eastern Europe.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:20.973
76,000 of these deportees were Jews.

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.050
And for Jews deportation in effect

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:26.560
meant a sentence of death.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.713
Very few returned, a couple
thousand, a little bit more.

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:33.873
But almost everybody who
was deported was murdered.

00:03:35.595 --> 00:03:40.595
For the others, many of these
were resistance fighters

00:03:41.583 --> 00:03:45.453
and their chances of
dying were high as well,

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:48.950
a little under 50%.

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:51.000
So there are two very
different experiences

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:55.570
as these mortality rates
attest of what the deportation

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:58.330
experience was like and yet in French,

00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:01.740
both experiences are
subsumed under a common term,

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.833
la déportation, the deportation.

00:04:06.250 --> 00:04:08.280
Immediately at the end of the war,

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:12.840
stories started to be told
about this experience.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:16.200
They were told in a variety of formats,

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:20.100
through a variety of
means and vehicles, books,

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:25.100
poetry, movies, artwork,
monuments, memorials of all kinds.

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:30.380
This right from the very start.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:33.523
And the people who told these
stories, fashioned them,

00:04:35.150 --> 00:04:36.560
came from a variety of backgrounds.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.420
Some were survivors, some
are relatives of survivors,

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:44.200
some were artists who thought
the experience urgent enough

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:46.150
that they felt they had to deal with it

00:04:47.182 --> 00:04:48.015
in one way or another.

00:04:48.015 --> 00:04:50.140
And I discuss a number
of these in my books

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:53.660
and I'll cite you a list,
it's longer than that,

00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:55.963
but some of the principle
ones, Robert Antelme,

00:04:57.238 --> 00:04:59.490
Pablo Picasso, I'll I Renee, John Keyhole,

00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:04.490
David Ruse, Elie Wiesel,
Andre Schwarz-Bart,

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:09.900
Claude Longspawn and others as well

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:14.900
whom I will mention in my
remarks as we go along.

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:19.260
So many media, many people,

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:20.853
it's an enormous outpouring.

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:25.110
I wind down my analysis
of this corpus of work

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:28.620
sometimes in the mid 1990s,
but it continues on that down

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:33.620
to the present days and
I'm still reading it.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:38.630
And I suspect I will be reading
it for many years to come.

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:40.530
These stories are patterned

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:44.290
by political and religious commitments.

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:46.110
The communists who were among the first

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:50.920
to get into the story
telling practice or game,

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:52.320
I don't wanna use that word,

00:05:53.577 --> 00:05:55.443
get into the practice of storytelling,

00:05:56.660 --> 00:06:00.340
told a story of anti-fascist struggle.

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:03.070
The non-communist left also entered

00:06:03.070 --> 00:06:04.670
the frayed not long thereafter

00:06:04.670 --> 00:06:07.610
and for them the concentration airy form

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:12.610
was not the open urge of
the fascist world alone,

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:17.520
but of totalitarians and generals,

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:20.320
Stalins as well as Hitler instead

00:06:21.444 --> 00:06:22.644
had concentration camps.

00:06:24.590 --> 00:06:28.210
For others, the concentration
reform was something

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:31.507
that was imminent in modern life itself.

00:06:31.507 --> 00:06:32.913
And you found it everywhere,

00:06:34.119 --> 00:06:36.480
not just in in Hitler's Germany

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:39.850
or Stalin's Soviet union,

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:43.050
but you found that even in France itself,

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:47.520
France maintaining interment
concentration camps

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:48.777
during the Algerian war.

00:06:52.746 --> 00:06:57.400
The galst also had a word to
say about camps and deportation

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:01.230
but they saw it through the
lens of an ongoing struggle

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:04.420
against a hereditary full Germany

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:07.500
and discussed this
experience in the framework

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:09.913
of what they called the 30 years war.

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:13.600
Now, as for religious commitments,

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:16.630
Roman Catholic deportees

00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:19.550
talked about their experience
as the fight of the spirit

00:07:20.799 --> 00:07:24.330
against the Nazi new paganism,
and Jews too had something

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:28.042
to say about the deportation
experience really

00:07:28.042 --> 00:07:31.320
from the right from the very beginning.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:33.000
It was seen in a variety of lights.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.480
And these first post-war decades

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:38.820
as part of a a millennial long experience

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:41.530
of persecution that dated
back to biblical times.

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:45.090
And from this perspective,
those who were murdered

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:48.397
in the deportation, murdered in the camps,

00:07:48.397 --> 00:07:53.290
had died as martyrs in
sanctification of the name.

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:55.060
Kidush Hasham.

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:59.200
But others stressed Jewish resistance

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:01.540
in the face of Nazi persecution.

00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:04.870
Aside in the case of the
Warsaw ghetto uprising,

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:09.370
but not just that, there were
also sonderkommando revolts

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:13.320
in various death camps at
Chelmno, Sobibor and Treblinka.

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:17.340
And these two were celebrated,
and these Jewish death

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:19.330
were understood to be not just martyrs,

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:20.737
but also humans.

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:26.550
So there is an ebb and flow to the stories

00:08:26.550 --> 00:08:28.470
told about the deportation,

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:32.053
a variety of themes and
narratives interweaving.

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:36.090
Try to make four points about these,

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:37.510
about this ebb and flow.

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:40.610
One is it's inflicted by events.

00:08:40.610 --> 00:08:43.530
The major events of
the year, the cold war,

00:08:43.530 --> 00:08:47.513
of course the Algerian war
which I've already mentioned.

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:53.550
But also the work of 1967,
the Arab Israeli war,

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:57.603
but the negation is crisis
the 1980s and 1990s.

00:08:58.930 --> 00:09:00.370
And I should add as well,

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:02.453
the De Gaulle's return to power in 1958.

00:09:07.069 --> 00:09:09.690
But, Jews were always a part of the story.

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:13.538
It used to be received wisdom
that there was a long silence.

00:09:13.538 --> 00:09:16.460
And of course in many
homes and many families,

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:19.010
that's true but I'm talking
about a public science.

00:09:20.288 --> 00:09:22.310
It used to be thought that
there was a public science

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:25.860
in the science and the Jewish
experience in Jewish suffering

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:27.440
was not not discussed.

00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:30.163
And I try to push back
against this a little bit

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:33.750
acknowledging isn't
the first word decades,

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:36.060
while there was a Jewish voice,

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:39.540
there were many many other
voices that were much loud

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:43.793
and occupied more space
in the public domain.

00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:48.230
All these voices, not quite a cacophony,

00:09:48.230 --> 00:09:50.830
but sometimes they
contended with each other,

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:53.550
competed with each other, and
fought against each other.

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:55.290
And the communist scholars struggle

00:09:55.290 --> 00:09:57.530
over deportation memories perhaps

00:09:57.530 --> 00:10:00.350
the most the most well-known.

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:03.790
And that's why I put memory
battles in the title of my book

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:06.922
because where there was cooperation,

00:10:06.922 --> 00:10:10.320
there was also contention.

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:12.340
Now the Jewish voice would grow stronger

00:10:13.325 --> 00:10:17.860
in the 1970s and 1980s,
and eventually become,

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:21.547
or the 1990s, the dominant one.

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:28.980
And as a result, historians
talk about a reversal of memory.

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:30.380
And as I see it, it's not just

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:32.120
that the Jewish voice becomes dominant,

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:35.980
but also the tonality of
the Jewish story itself

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:40.720
begins to change with
the focus less on heroism

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:45.720
and on modernism and more
on the experience of loss.

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:51.350
The emphasis is no longer
on sacrifice, more on loss,

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:53.863
and sometimes also on victimization.

00:10:54.900 --> 00:10:57.030
All of these of course begs the question

00:10:57.030 --> 00:11:00.683
as to why such a reversal
of memories took place.

00:11:01.720 --> 00:11:04.420
The answer to this and
others have addressed it,

00:11:04.420 --> 00:11:09.160
the question, my answer
is a little different.

00:11:09.160 --> 00:11:11.740
Often the practice is to emphasize

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:15.873
very particular about the
Eichmann trial of 1961.

00:11:17.890 --> 00:11:21.750
Which was sort of turned a light
on in public consciousness.

00:11:21.750 --> 00:11:26.750
Or the Arab Israeli war
of 1867, 1967 forgive me.

00:11:27.260 --> 00:11:28.990
And I do treat these events,

00:11:28.990 --> 00:11:31.270
but I interpret them
less as turning points

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:34.720
than as the accelerators
pushing a process forward

00:11:34.720 --> 00:11:37.920
that had its origins in elsewhere

00:11:42.868 --> 00:11:43.701
with other kinds of explanations.

00:11:43.701 --> 00:11:47.640
And I insist on two vectors
of change in particular,

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:50.270
the first, once again not unique to me

00:11:50.270 --> 00:11:55.060
but I put a lot of weight on
it is Catholic Jewish dialogue,

00:11:55.060 --> 00:11:59.413
Christian Jewish dialogue,
Catholic in particular.

00:12:01.940 --> 00:12:05.860
So, Catholics play a
prominent role in my story

00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:09.750
of two kinds, one is
brokers or intermediaries

00:12:09.750 --> 00:12:13.760
as possible who helped
bring the Jewish story

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:15.320
to public attention.

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:18.539
And here I would cite
the case of Jack Maritan

00:12:18.539 --> 00:12:21.400
who was a very good
friend of Marc Chagall's.

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:23.950
Collected some of Chagall's work,

00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:27.840
or Frostmot Mobiack who
provided the introduction

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:29.022
to Elie Wiesel's (speaking
foreign language)

00:12:29.022 --> 00:12:31.220
when it was published

00:12:31.220 --> 00:12:34.280
in French in 1958.

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:38.010
And also lesser known for
you, a man named Paul Flamo,

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:39.897
who was the editor at Lesoi.

00:12:39.897 --> 00:12:42.040
But Lesoi was the publishing house

00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:44.580
that first published
Andre Schrafft's Butt's

00:12:45.673 --> 00:12:47.900
(speaking in foreign language)

00:12:47.900 --> 00:12:50.870
in 1959, which was a
pre gold corp winning.

00:12:50.870 --> 00:12:52.950
A very prestigious prize,

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:56.600
Plumber published Elie
Wiesel after one week.

00:12:56.600 --> 00:12:58.280
And he was also the publisher of some of

00:12:58.280 --> 00:13:00.700
Saul Friedlander's first works.

00:13:00.700 --> 00:13:02.187
And some of you in the audience

00:13:02.187 --> 00:13:05.170
who will know Saul Friedlander's

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:07.620
spectacular work on the Holocaust,

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:09.483
the recent to value study.

00:13:11.330 --> 00:13:14.460
But it wasn't all about dialogue.

00:13:14.460 --> 00:13:16.160
There was also a lot of contention

00:13:17.101 --> 00:13:19.790
between Christians and Jews,
between Catholics and Jews.

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:24.790
And this to royal the public domain.

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:28.340
And this connection I
would cite three things.

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:30.580
First of all, the work of Julie Zack,

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:35.580
some of you may know, wrote
a groundbreaking hefty texts

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:38.240
which was published in late 40s.

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:43.240
Jay Xu in Israel, he excoriates the church

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:46.180
for what he calls the
teaching of contempt.

00:13:46.180 --> 00:13:49.820
The teaching of anti-Semitism
across millennia,

00:13:49.820 --> 00:13:54.820
the teaching bit in Isaac's
polemic grease the wheels

00:13:57.523 --> 00:14:01.563
that led train loads of Jews to Auschwitz

00:14:04.100 --> 00:14:05.220
Not just Isaac,

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:09.080
there's also the family
affair, which erupted

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:12.700
in public life in 1952 and 1953.

00:14:12.700 --> 00:14:14.500
I don't wanna rehearse it in detail,

00:14:16.028 --> 00:14:18.120
but two Jewish boys had been placed

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:21.220
for safekeeping during
the war, by their parents

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:22.910
with a Catholic religious order.

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:26.373
The parents were deported and murdered.

00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:28.840
The children survived,

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:30.950
looked after by this religious order

00:14:30.950 --> 00:14:32.950
who farmed them out to a nurse.

00:14:32.950 --> 00:14:36.113
And after the war, an aunt
came looking for them,

00:14:36.950 --> 00:14:41.010
and the caretaker had
the children baptized

00:14:41.010 --> 00:14:44.000
and the church did not want
to let go of the children

00:14:44.000 --> 00:14:46.340
in effect kidnapping them.

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:48.920
And there was a major, major public uproar

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:52.030
that was not just
national but international

00:14:52.030 --> 00:14:53.233
at the same time.

00:14:54.310 --> 00:14:57.810
Very contentious, in the
end the boys were released

00:14:57.810 --> 00:15:00.710
to their aunt and the family, the remnants

00:15:00.710 --> 00:15:04.443
of the (indistinct)
family moved to Israel.

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:10.420
I treat this as a scene setter
for the Catholic churches,

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:15.233
Vatican too reflections on
its teaching on the Jews,

00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:18.433
and at Vatican too.

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:24.320
It people a declaration follows,
called no strike a tattoo

00:15:26.064 --> 00:15:28.700
in our times, which represents a beginning

00:15:29.664 --> 00:15:32.670
of rethinking church
doctrine on Jewish life.

00:15:32.670 --> 00:15:35.867
But the Catholic Jewish
dialogue plays a major role

