because of the narrative
power of this particular work

00:46:34.400 --> 00:46:39.400
and the darkness that it sheds.

00:46:39.730 --> 00:46:42.230
Oh, and on the matter
of time, I was thinking,

00:46:42.230 --> 00:46:45.170
I'm sorry to revert to an
earlier question cause it...

00:46:45.170 --> 00:46:46.770
I think one of the things that just

00:46:46.770 --> 00:46:48.060
it takes time to tell the story

00:46:48.060 --> 00:46:50.330
but stories build up over time.

00:46:50.330 --> 00:46:52.900
So that what is the Holocaust?

00:46:52.900 --> 00:46:57.900
Of course its not known in 1944, 1945.

00:46:58.370 --> 00:47:01.553
And it requires really decades of work.

00:47:02.670 --> 00:47:06.750
That is both scholarly
and testimonial in nature

00:47:06.750 --> 00:47:10.197
for the various aspects and
dimensions of this thing,

00:47:10.197 --> 00:47:15.197
of this monstrosity to start
to come into focus for people.

00:47:15.600 --> 00:47:20.600
So in that sense, this is
another temporal dimension

00:47:20.887 --> 00:47:23.490
to the story I have to tell.

00:47:23.490 --> 00:47:25.870
- So, we have so many great questions here

00:47:25.870 --> 00:47:27.530
and I'm gonna apologize

00:47:27.530 --> 00:47:28.870
for not being able to ask all of them.

00:47:28.870 --> 00:47:30.510
And I'm just gonna skip to some things

00:47:30.510 --> 00:47:32.930
that we haven't touched on but
I think are really important.

00:47:32.930 --> 00:47:36.930
So there's a question from
Sam Moen, from our colleague,

00:47:36.930 --> 00:47:41.930
about what's at stake in
focusing on writing French

00:47:43.910 --> 00:47:46.377
across the genres, versus for instance

00:47:46.377 --> 00:47:50.302
some of the early writing in Yiddish,

00:47:50.302 --> 00:47:54.380
and speaking in Yiddish
about the deportation.

00:47:54.380 --> 00:47:57.880
- So two things, one is
I don't do the Yiddish.

00:47:57.880 --> 00:47:59.930
So that is an obstacle.

00:47:59.930 --> 00:48:04.080
I got help translating
Elie Wiesel's, Night,

00:48:04.080 --> 00:48:07.800
when it first was published in Yiddish

00:48:07.800 --> 00:48:10.290
and I was interested in the translation

00:48:10.290 --> 00:48:11.840
from French anteaters.

00:48:11.840 --> 00:48:15.400
But second of all, and so this isn't just,

00:48:15.400 --> 00:48:19.290
I don't have the skills,
it's also I want it

00:48:19.290 --> 00:48:22.020
in the domain of putt French public life

00:48:22.020 --> 00:48:25.270
and Yiddish is not the linguistic means

00:48:25.270 --> 00:48:27.400
by which that will happen.

00:48:27.400 --> 00:48:30.350
It has to happen in French.

00:48:30.350 --> 00:48:34.630
And so that's why the focus
is on the French language,

00:48:34.630 --> 00:48:36.903
the French language corporates.

00:48:36.903 --> 00:48:38.730
It's not that I'm not interested

00:48:38.730 --> 00:48:43.170
to quite a wonderful book by
a young man named Simo Parago

00:48:43.170 --> 00:48:46.600
whom you, I don't know
if you all know him,

00:48:46.600 --> 00:48:48.740
but I know Carolyn knows him.

00:48:48.740 --> 00:48:53.156
Which is about the Parisian, Jewish world

00:48:53.156 --> 00:48:56.620
largely or significantly Yiddish speaking.

00:48:56.620 --> 00:49:00.380
And the way in which the
experience in memorialization

00:49:00.380 --> 00:49:04.720
and mourning of loss is
present in a very powerful way,

00:49:04.720 --> 00:49:07.883
almost in a constitutive
way after World War II.

00:49:09.220 --> 00:49:12.980
So if you're interested
in that, there it is.

00:49:12.980 --> 00:49:14.833
And he does it beautifully.

00:49:16.107 --> 00:49:21.107
But I want it when the
world beyond the borders

00:49:21.330 --> 00:49:23.990
to the Jewish community
is made to pay attention,

00:49:23.990 --> 00:49:25.793
or is led to pay attention.

00:49:26.670 --> 00:49:29.750
And so I think that the
medium for that to happen

00:49:29.750 --> 00:49:31.833
is in the French language itself.

00:49:32.790 --> 00:49:36.650
- So here's a question from Ron Levy about

00:49:36.650 --> 00:49:40.720
how the memory of the
deportation intersects

00:49:40.720 --> 00:49:44.490
with other memories,
for instance of Algeria

00:49:44.490 --> 00:49:46.373
and the Algerian war.

00:49:47.870 --> 00:49:50.883
- So that's a great question.

00:49:52.167 --> 00:49:55.390
So this is Michael Rothberg
has written extensively

00:49:55.390 --> 00:49:59.314
and inventively, imaginatively about this.

00:49:59.314 --> 00:50:02.410
What I would have to add to it

00:50:02.410 --> 00:50:06.413
is that some of it depends
on what your politics are.

00:50:07.620 --> 00:50:09.873
And so for the non-communist left,

00:50:10.820 --> 00:50:15.820
the experience of the Algerian
war is a wake up call.

00:50:16.400 --> 00:50:18.433
They are galvanized by this.

00:50:19.820 --> 00:50:24.690
Xstore deportees, I mean,
someone like Charlotte Delbelt,

00:50:25.590 --> 00:50:29.440
it's a good case in point,
she was once a party member.

00:50:29.440 --> 00:50:33.800
She leaves the party, she's
ferociously anti-colonial

00:50:33.800 --> 00:50:36.360
and she sees her through the experience,

00:50:36.360 --> 00:50:38.720
through the lens of her
own deportation experience

00:50:38.720 --> 00:50:40.040
to Auschwitz.

00:50:40.040 --> 00:50:41.973
And that fuels her passion.

00:50:42.840 --> 00:50:45.770
Now, there are other
people like German Tio

00:50:45.770 --> 00:50:50.770
who has been patronized
for whom the Algerian war

00:50:51.220 --> 00:50:54.163
is also a crushing experience.

00:50:55.070 --> 00:50:57.860
And she wants to find a solution to it.

00:50:57.860 --> 00:51:00.840
And she sees the war and torture

00:51:00.840 --> 00:51:02.340
through the lens of her own experience,

00:51:02.340 --> 00:51:05.730
but she doesn't come out
for an independent Algeria

00:51:05.730 --> 00:51:07.830
at the far end.

00:51:07.830 --> 00:51:12.243
So in both cases, the war
plays an important role

00:51:12.243 --> 00:51:15.530
in mobilizing them,

00:51:15.530 --> 00:51:19.623
and their work experience
shapes how they're mobilized.

00:51:20.670 --> 00:51:23.360
But they come out at
slightly different places,

00:51:23.360 --> 00:51:25.053
just because you intersect,

00:51:26.010 --> 00:51:28.960
doesn't mean that you're gonna come out

00:51:28.960 --> 00:51:30.850
with the same view of the Algerian war.

00:51:30.850 --> 00:51:33.520
Some of that depends on your politics,

00:51:33.520 --> 00:51:38.520
and Delvaux is a non-communist
leftist, very left wing.

00:51:39.390 --> 00:51:41.680
In German Tiana is a Gaullist.

00:51:41.680 --> 00:51:45.527
And so they've come out both
shaped by the Algerian war

00:51:45.527 --> 00:51:48.300
and the connection or intersectionality.

00:51:48.300 --> 00:51:49.133
That's not the word.

00:51:49.133 --> 00:51:51.660
Multidimensionality, that's the word.

00:51:51.660 --> 00:51:52.830
Between these two experiences,

00:51:52.830 --> 00:51:54.120
but they come out at different places

00:51:54.120 --> 00:51:56.423
cause they enter them
politically differently.

00:51:58.190 --> 00:52:01.090
