00:54:20.910 --> 00:54:23.061
was erected by communists.

00:54:23.061 --> 00:54:26.750
In 1995 the plaque that
you see is added there

00:54:26.750 --> 00:54:29.600
that identifies the people
who died at Auschwitz

00:54:29.600 --> 00:54:31.850
as being principally Jews.

00:54:31.850 --> 00:54:35.000
That's only edited in mid 1990s.

00:54:35.000 --> 00:54:38.090
So I have a feeling that
this sort of local story,

00:54:38.090 --> 00:54:41.080
and a local story where
Jews are identified by name

00:54:41.990 --> 00:54:45.480
is last 30 years story.

00:54:45.480 --> 00:54:48.543
A little bit after or where I wind up.

00:54:50.310 --> 00:54:52.250
- So here's a really interesting question

00:54:52.250 --> 00:54:57.210
about the role of the organized
Jewish community in France,

00:54:57.210 --> 00:54:58.270
in controlling memory.

00:54:58.270 --> 00:54:59.467
So like the (speaking
in foreign language),

00:55:00.642 --> 00:55:03.290
did they try to limit the importance

00:55:03.290 --> 00:55:06.440
of the Jewish component of the deportation

00:55:06.440 --> 00:55:09.710
out of fear that it
would arouse antisemitism

00:55:09.710 --> 00:55:12.710
or that they'd be accused
of communal charisma

00:55:12.710 --> 00:55:13.760
and things like that.

00:55:15.940 --> 00:55:18.200
- This is, it's an interesting...

00:55:18.200 --> 00:55:20.640
I'll tell you what I have on that.

00:55:20.640 --> 00:55:24.410
Which was the cost to our
early puts up a plaque

00:55:24.410 --> 00:55:26.660
in the (speaking foreign
language) synagogue.

00:55:27.769 --> 00:55:29.070
It's in the peristyle there,

00:55:29.070 --> 00:55:31.660
which talks about all who were lost,

00:55:31.660 --> 00:55:33.460
but it's actually very reticent

00:55:33.460 --> 00:55:36.520
about identifying the
people who died as Jews.

00:55:36.520 --> 00:55:41.010
Now it's in the synagogue
at the same time.

00:55:41.010 --> 00:55:42.890
So it's a little bit hidden away.

00:55:42.890 --> 00:55:47.890
And when Isaac Snelson in this
(speaking foreign language)

00:55:48.750 --> 00:55:52.190
wants to build a monument
that (indistinct)

00:55:53.394 --> 00:55:55.100
has to go in alone.

00:55:55.100 --> 00:55:57.770
And he's very creative, very dynamic.

00:55:57.770 --> 00:56:00.590
So there's some reticence about that,

00:56:00.590 --> 00:56:03.840
but not completely, because the consistoire

00:56:03.840 --> 00:56:07.580
does get ashes or soil
from concentration camps

00:56:07.580 --> 00:56:10.560
and distributes them around
France for memorial purposes.

00:56:10.560 --> 00:56:12.070
It has use career services,

00:56:12.070 --> 00:56:14.820
it has Memorial Memorial services.

00:56:14.820 --> 00:56:18.670
So while they're low
key and not insistent,

00:56:18.670 --> 00:56:19.770
they're not in denial.

00:56:19.770 --> 00:56:22.730
And it's an interesting phenomenon,

00:56:22.730 --> 00:56:26.690
France has something called
the (speaking foreign language)

00:56:26.690 --> 00:56:31.110
every April, a deportation Memorial day.

00:56:31.110 --> 00:56:36.110
And there's an itinerary,
various stops along the way.

00:56:36.722 --> 00:56:40.330
It was a very smallest shift
fair when it began in 1954

00:56:40.330 --> 00:56:44.810
but by 1955, '56, the
Rue de la noir to our synagogue

00:56:44.810 --> 00:56:46.820
figured as part of this.

00:56:46.820 --> 00:56:50.310
As well as Notre Dame, as
well as Shoah Memorial,

00:56:50.310 --> 00:56:52.930
as well as a number of
other memorials as well.

00:56:52.930 --> 00:56:57.930
And so even at this early
stage, in the mid 50s we are,

00:56:58.060 --> 00:57:01.020
you have the Jewish story
with the call ceased war

00:57:02.040 --> 00:57:06.080
included part of the general itinerary

00:57:07.650 --> 00:57:09.600
for memorialization of the deportation.

00:57:11.729 --> 00:57:13.370
- So we're almost at
the time, but there are

